    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d294">

                <pb facs="0290" n="174" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg174"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.00/607.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Chargé d’Affaires to the Secretary of
                        State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1911-10-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 26, 1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The rebel commanding general, Li, informed consuls October 22 that he will
                    confiscate ships carrying contraband, and gave a list of goods to be considered
                    contraband. The Dean of the diplomatic corps suggests that although General Li
                    has no power to confiscate foreign ships carrying articles not prohibited by
                    treaty with China, foreign shipping companies should nevertheless be advised to
                    abstain from trade in munitions of war until the situation clears. May I concur?
                    Consular body has informed General Li that they have taken notice of his
                    communication.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Williams.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
